\title{\code{\verb~|perf/loop-unfolded-once~}: equal terms that are two different closures}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{arena}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/AR1.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{An arena performance test that the official kernel answers by structural identity of two instantiated terms, and on which a machine over values has two closures of different terms that are equal only after substitution. Source: the arena dry run [rep-AR1](rep-AR1), table 0.1, and [design-switches-document](design-switches-document); [the profiling session](ses-JRSD).}
\pre{\verb~|
Nat.rec M Z S1 m  =?=  Nat.rec M Z S2 m
-- S1 = fun k _ => p (lo + k)               under  lo := 0, p := a closure
-- S2 = fun k _ => (fun i => g w i) (0 + k)
-- officially S1 and S2 are the same term once instantiated
~}
\table{
\tr{\th{Configuration}\th{Outcome}\th{Cost}}
\tr{\td{reference engine}\td{accept}\td{76 ms, 1.5 G instructions, 60 MiB}}
\tr{\td{machine, \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}}\td{accept after the machine gives up at its own step bound and the reference engine decides}\td{31.6 s, 199.2 G instructions, 8,686 MiB, alone in a quiet window; killed at the limit of 40 s in the suite run}}
\tr{\td{machine with the candidate set that contains \code{\verb~|same~}}\td{accept}\td{75 ms, 0.77 G instructions}}
\tr{\td{machine with the two shortcuts}\td{accept}\td{58 ms, 0.85 G instructions}}
}
\ul{
\li{Measured in an arena-shaped container on the aarch64 development host. The arena runner is slower, so with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} this test is a likely timeout there, not a wrong verdict.}
\li{The plain machine cannot accept it by an exact, bounded structural test: it needs the comparison of the slots of the two closures. The walk \code{\verb~|same~}, which answers when two values read back to one expression, settles it.}
}
\p{See also: [staging switches and the default switch set](design-switches).}
